        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Recent work in bone biology field has demonstrated that cellular damage due to molecules called reactive oxygen species (so-called oxidative damage) is a key mechanism contributing to aging-related bone loss, as well as to that occurring with the loss of estrogen after menopause. We know very little about whether the loss of gravity as experienced in the space environment modulates oxidative damage to bone. A newly recognized source of oxidative damage in bone is that due to iron overload, or an increase in body iron stores, as has now been demonstrated in both ISS crew members. Data published this month indicate that sub-pathological levels of serum ferritin (a marker for iron stores) contribute to accelerated bone loss and to incident vertebral fractures in healthy middle-aged men and women. We have preliminary data indicating that similar moderate elevations in body iron stores, as induced by a high iron diet, can result in loss of cancellous bone and diminished bone formation activity in rodents. The experiments in this proposal will utilize iron overload as an experimental intervention to exacerbate oxidative damage during a period of simulated microgravity (by hindlimb unloading in male and female mice) to define the gender-specific mechanistic role of oxidative damage as a key regulator of bone mass and, ultimately, fracture risk as modulated by the gravitational unloading of the space environment. In a final specific aim, we will test whether superimposing acute and continuous (over 42 days) radiation exposure exacerbates oxidative damage and bone decrements during hindlimb unloading. These experiments are deliberately designed to enable a rapid transition to flight experiments requiring minimal intervention during flight aboard ISS, should these ground-based simulations yield provocative findings to justify flight experiments. Iron overload can be maintained by dietary intake, and limiting the acute radiation exposure in Experiment 2 to just before the unloading period allows for testing of the same hypotheses in a flight experiment. The experiments outlined in this proposal addresses how microgravity affects key regulatory mechanisms governing alterations in bone (from OCB-2 in the Space Biology Plan) and also one of the NRC Decadal Survey's highest priority recommendations, to expand our understanding of gender differences in adaptation to the space environment, particularly in the area of bone and long-term radiation risks. <P>

APPROACH: <br>
The first specific aim of this project is to comprehensively define the impact of increasing iron stores (“iron overload”) on the bone response to microgravity, as simulated in a mouse model of hindlimb unloading (HU).   We hypothesize that bone loss will be exacerbated and that the mechanism for this will be oxidative damage caused by iron overload.   Further, we will be able to detect sex differences by completing these experiments in both male and female mice.   Comprehensive testing of iron status and oxidative damage, both systemically and tissue-specific, will enable identification of mechanisms for the changes in bone integrity observed in all specific aims.<br><br>  

The second specific aim is to test our hypothesis that administration of n-acetylcysteine (NAC), a potent inhibitor of oxidative damage, will mitigate deleterious effects of iron overload on bone loss during HU.    If it does, we then have evidence that a major mechanism for iron overload effect is due to exacerbation of oxidative damage.   For this experiment we will use the one sex (male or female) that exhibited the largest response to iron overload in the first specific aim.<br><br>  

The third specific aim involves testing the added impact of very low-dose continuous radiation exposure during a period of HU in mice with normal and high iron stores.   There is growing evidence that space-relevant levels of radiation initiate oxidative damage in the exposed tissues; hence we hypothesize that bone loss will be further exacerbated by the interactions of unweighting (HU), iron overload, and radiation exposure.  We will use the one sex (male or female) for these experiments that exhibits the largest response to combined iron overload and HU in the initial specific aim.<p>   


RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is currently in progress. Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
